# Real-World Incident: Spring Security AuthenticatedVoter Bypass (CVE-2024-22257)

**Timeline**: March 18, 2024
**Severity**: HIGH (CVSS 7.5)
**Impact**: Broken access control - unauthenticated users bypass authorization
**Scope**: Spring Security 5.7.0 through 6.2.2
**Fix**: Immediate patches released for all affected versions

## Yes - You Have a Critical Vulnerability

If your application uses Spring Security's `AuthenticatedVoter` directly in authorization decisions, you are vulnerable to broken access control. This is especially common in legacy code that hasn't migrated to the newer `AuthorizationManager` API.

### Root Cause: Null Authentication Bypass

Spring Security's `AuthenticatedVoter#vote()` method has a logic flaw: when passed a `null` Authentication object, it returns `ACCESS_GRANTED (true)` instead of the correct `ACCESS_DENIED (false)`.

This means:
- Unauthenticated requests with `null` Auth object grant access
- OAuth2 tokens with expired/invalid claims grant access
- Null principal objects bypass authorization checks

### Vulnerable Implementation

```java
// VULNERABLE: Direct use of AuthenticatedVoter
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class VulnerableOAuth2SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(authz -> authz
                .requestMatchers("/api/admin/**").authenticated()  // Requires any authentication
                .requestMatchers("/api/public/**").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .oauth2Login()
            .and()
            .build();

        return http.build();
    }

    // VULNERABLE: Custom access decision voter using AuthenticatedVoter
    @Bean
    public AccessDecisionManager accessDecisionManager() {
        List<AccessDecisionVoter<?>> voters = new ArrayList<>();

        // VULNERABLE: Directly instantiating and using deprecated AuthenticatedVoter
        // This voter has the null Authentication bug
        voters.add(new AuthenticatedVoter());
        voters.add(new RoleVoter());
        voters.add(new WebExpressionVoter());

        // VULNERABLE: Using deprecated AccessDecisionManager
        // If AuthenticatedVoter returns true for null auth, access is granted
        return new AffirmativeBased(voters);
    }
}

@Component
public class AdminController {

    @Autowired
    private AccessDecisionManager accessDecisionManager;

    @GetMapping("/api/admin/users")
    @PreAuthorize("authenticated")  // Should require authentication
    public ResponseEntity<List<User>> getAllUsers(
            @AuthenticationPrincipal OAuth2User principal) {

        // VULNERABLE: If principal is null (unauthenticated), 
        // AuthenticatedVoter#vote() returns true, allowing access
        if (principal == null) {
            // This check doesn't help - AccessDecisionManager already voted true
            return ResponseEntity.status(401).build();
        }

        return ResponseEntity.ok(userService.findAll());
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/api/admin/users/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> deleteUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
        // If unauthenticated request reaches here, AuthenticatedVoter
        // may have incorrectly granted access via null Authentication
        userService.deleteById(id);
        return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
    }
}
```

### Attack Demonstration

**Stage 1: Unauthenticated request to protected endpoint**

```bash
# Attacker makes request WITHOUT OAuth2 token
curl -X GET "https://vulnerable-app.com/api/admin/users"

# Request arrives at Spring Security filter chain
# Spring creates SecurityContext with null Authentication (not authenticated)
# @PreAuthorize("authenticated") evaluates
# AuthenticatedVoter#vote(null, request, required_AUTHENTICATION) is called
```

**Stage 2: AuthenticatedVoter incorrectly returns ACCESS_GRANTED**

```java
// Inside Spring Security's AuthenticatedVoter (VULNERABLE CODE)
public class AuthenticatedVoter implements AccessDecisionVoter<Object> {

    @Override
    public int vote(Authentication authentication, Object object,
            Collection<ConfigAttribute> attributes) {

        int result = ACCESS_ABSTAIN;  // Default: don't vote

        for (ConfigAttribute attribute : attributes) {
            if (AUTHENTICATED.equals(attribute)) {
                // BUG: When authentication is null, this returns true!
                // Correct behavior: return ACCESS_DENIED when authentication is null
                // Actual behavior: continues and returns ACCESS_GRANTED
                result = ACCESS_GRANTED;  // WRONG! Should check if auth is null
                break;
            }
            if (DENY_ALL.equals(attribute)) {
                return ACCESS_DENIED;
            }
        }

        return result;  // Returns GRANTED even when authentication is null
    }
}

// FIX (what it SHOULD be):
public int vote(Authentication authentication, Object object,
        Collection<ConfigAttribute> attributes) {

    int result = ACCESS_ABSTAIN;

    for (ConfigAttribute attribute : attributes) {
        if (AUTHENTICATED.equals(attribute)) {
            // FIXED: Check if authentication is null FIRST
            if (authentication == null) {
                return ACCESS_DENIED;  // No token = not authenticated
            }
            result = ACCESS_GRANTED;
            break;
        }
        if (DENY_ALL.equals(attribute)) {
            return ACCESS_DENIED;
        }
    }

    return result;
}
```

**Stage 3: Attacker gains unauthorized access**

```bash
# Spring Security says: "ACCESS_GRANTED" (via buggy AuthenticatedVoter)
# Response: 200 OK with sensitive data
{
  "users": [
    {"id": 1, "email": "admin@company.com", "roles": ["ADMIN"]},
    {"id": 2, "email": "user@company.com", "roles": ["USER"]},
    ...
  ]
}

# Attacker now has:
# - Email addresses of all users
# - User IDs for further exploitation
# - Role information for privilege escalation

# Attacker can continue attacking:
curl -X DELETE "https://vulnerable-app.com/api/admin/users/1"
# Deletes the admin account without authentication!
```

## Common Mistakes

1. **Client-side authorization checks only** — Hiding UI elements but not enforcing access control on the server. Attackers bypass the frontend entirely with direct API calls.

2. **Insecure Direct Object References (IDOR)** — Using sequential IDs (user/1, user/2) without verifying the requesting user owns the resource. Always validate resource ownership server-side.

3. **Missing function-level access control** — Checking authentication but not authorization. A logged-in regular user should not access admin endpoints just because they are authenticated.

4. **Trusting JWT claims without verification** — Reading role or permissions from JWT payload without verifying the signature, or accepting tokens from untrusted issuers.

## Secure Implementation

### Tier 1: Critical Emergency Fix (5 minutes)
**IMMEDIATE: Upgrade Spring Security to patched version**

The vulnerability is in Spring Security's AuthenticatedVoter itself. Upgrade immediately:

```xml
<!-- pom.xml -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-security-core</artifactId>
    <!-- VULNERABLE versions -->
    <!-- 5.7.0-5.7.11, 5.8.0-5.8.10, 6.0.0-6.0.9, 6.1.0-6.1.7, 6.2.0-6.2.2 -->

    <!-- PATCHED versions (use MINIMUM) -->
    <version>6.2.3</version>  <!-- Or 6.1.8, 6.0.10, 5.8.11, 5.7.12 -->
</dependency>

<!-- If using Spring Boot, also upgrade Spring Boot -->
<parent>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
    <version>3.2.3</version>  <!-- Includes patched Spring Security 6.2.3+ -->
</parent>
```

Verify patch:

```bash
mvn dependency:tree | grep spring-security-core
# Must show 5.7.12+, 5.8.11+, 6.0.10+, 6.1.8+, 6.2.3+ or higher
```

**Status: Tier 1 complete in <5 minutes. AuthenticatedVoter bug is patched.**

